        "chapterId": 67,
        "headingId": 375,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Ovary</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:03:08"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61958,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 67,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "<p>Most ovarian fibromas are unilateral, but occasional cases are bilateral, most commonly in young patients with naevoid basal cell carcinoma syndrome (Gorlin syndrome) {3547011}. Patients may present with symptoms attributable to an ovarian mass or occasionally with hormonal manifestations; Meigs syndrome (ascites and pleural effusion) occurs in a small proportion of cases.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-02T17:55:50"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61959,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 67,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Fibroma is the most common ovarian stromal tumour, accounting for 4% of all ovarian neoplasms in clinicopathological studies. Fibromas occur in approximately 75% of female patients with naevoid basal cell carcinoma syndrome {3547011}. Fibroma may occur at any age; it most frequently arises in middle age (average patient age: 48&nbsp;years) and less commonly before the age of 30&nbsp;years {7260859}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:03:08"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61960,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 67,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:03:08"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61961,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 67,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Trisomy and/or tetrasomy&nbsp;12 is often found, and rarely <em>IDH1</em> mutation {11852357; 23347143}. In cellular fibromas, loss of heterozygosity at 9q22.3 (<em>PTCH1</em>) and 19p13.3 (<em>STK11</em>) is frequent {16084949}. <em>FOXL2</em> mutations are absent {23774170}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:03:08"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61962,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 67,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The ovarian capsule is usually smooth, with a hard, chalky, white or yellowish-white cut surface; cellular tumours may be tan and soft. Areas of oedema, cystic degeneration, haemorrhage, or necrosis may be present.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:03:08"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61963,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 67,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Fibromas are composed of intersecting fascicles of cells with bland, spindled to ovoid nuclei and scant cytoplasm within a variably collagenous stroma, sometimes including hyalinized plaques {7260859}. Calcifications may be present, and rare tumours contain eosinophilic hyaline globules {19483628} or melanin pigment {29140884}. Mitoses are uncommon in most cases. Approximately 10% of fibromas are densely cellular; when only mild nuclear atypia is present, such cases are referred to as cellular fibromas, and they may exhibit high mitotic activity (mitotically active cellular fibroma) {16861962}. Haemorrhage and infarct-type necrosis may occur and should not be misinterpreted as coagulative tumour cell necrosis. Rarely, luteinized cells or a minor component (&lt;&nbsp;10%) of sex cord elements is seen. Fibromas may be immunoreactive for inhibin, calretinin, WT1, FOXL2, CD56, SF1, and hormone receptors; positive staining with inhibin and calretinin is often focal and/or weak {9688187; 12808064}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:03:08"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61964,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 67,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:03:08"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
